zPotential # Closed non-displaced fibula shaft fracture from twisting injury to lower leg
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Focal lateral shaft point tenderness, Positive proximal squeeze test, Preserved weight-bearing ability post-twist

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-045
Reason for Visit: Acute localized lower leg pain following a twisting injury, requiring urgent clinical assessment for an unstable tibiofibular fracture or compartment syndrome.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute rotational mechanism causing focal lateral leg pain. Urgent bedside evaluation was necessary to clear a potentially unstable distal tibia-fibula fracture, perform the Ottawa Ankle/Leg Rules, and definitively rule out acute compartment syndrome prior to allowing the patient to bear weight overnight.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I twisted my leg awkwardly stepping down, and now the outside of my calf is aching sharply when I squeeze it.
History of Present Illness: Patient provides a history of a sudden rotational or twisting force applied to the weight-bearing lower limb 2 hours ago. This resulted in focal point tenderness sharply localized along the lateral aspect of the mid-to-distal lower leg. The patient notes pain that is mechanically reproduced by squeezing the tibia and fibula together proximally. There is mild localized soft tissue swelling and delayed ecchymosis over the lateral compartment. The patient presents with a surprising ability to bear weight and walk, albeit with a noticeable antalgic limp.
Functional Status: Ambulating independently but with a deliberate antalgic limp favoring the non-injured side.
Social/Safety Concerns: Stairs present a minor hazard due to impaired weight-bearing confidence.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/80 | HR: 82 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 84 Kg
General: Alert, in mild discomfort when mobilizing, sitting with the leg resting.
Physical Exam: Lower leg examination reveals focal lateral shaft point tenderness precisely localized over the fibula. There is a complete absence of gross clinical deformity or obvious angulation of the leg. A positive proximal squeeze test mechanically reproduces the lateral pain. Intact active ankle dorsiflexion and plantarflexion are present with only mild discomfort. There is a complete absence of tense, unyielding calf swelling, with all four compartments remaining soft and compressible. Normal distal pedal pulses and intact sensation in the superficial and deep peroneal nerve distributions confirm intact neurovascular status.
Environmental Observations: Uneven garden step identified as the hazard.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Potential closed non-displaced fibula shaft fracture - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Tibial shaft fracture - Ruled out due to: Absence of focal point tenderness over the medial tibial crest and preserved, unassisted weight-bearing ability.

Acute compartment syndrome - Ruled out due to: Soft muscular compartments, absence of pain out of proportion to passive toe stretch, and intact deep peroneal sensation in the first web space.

High ankle (syndesmotic) sprain - Ruled out due to: Negative external rotation stress test, though the positive squeeze test necessitates ruling out a proximal fibular fracture (Maisonneuve).
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

A rotational injury to the lower limb causing lateral pain carries a significant risk for an isolated fibular fracture. Bedside clinical assessment confidently ruled out acute compartment syndrome and a major weight-bearing tibial fracture, establishing that the limb was neurovascularly intact. Urgent after-hours assessment was clinically required to clear these limb-threatening emergencies, institute safe weight-bearing protocols, and provide a protective compression bandage, ensuring the patient was safe to manage conservatively overnight until definitive full-length plain radiography could be performed the next morning.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Applied a tubular compression bandage and advised RICE therapy. Prescribed short-term oral analgesia. Explicitly instructed the patient to obtain full-length anteroposterior and lateral tibia-fibula radiographs tomorrow to definitively evaluate for a singular non-displaced fracture line through the fibula.
Positive Behavioral Change: Use a compression bandage during the day and elevate the leg when resting. This physically controls localized bleeding and hydrostatic pressure around the suspected fracture site, rapidly decreasing the throbbing pain and making it significantly easier to walk.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Tubular compression bandage.
Follow-up: Routine GP review tomorrow with the requested x-ray results to confirm the diagnosis and arrange orthopedic referral if required.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Proceed to the Emergency Department immediately if the calf suddenly becomes rock-hard, shiny, and agonizingly painful, if the foot turns cold or pale, or if you completely lose the ability to put any weight on the leg.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The fibula is a non-weight-bearing bone (bearing only 10% of body weight), meaning patients can often walk despite a complete fracture; an x-ray is mandatory.
Clinical Course: If a simple non-displaced fibula fracture is confirmed, it is generally treated conservatively in a supportive boot and heals over 6 weeks.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical attention if you lose feeling between your big toe and second toe, or if the leg pain suddenly becomes unbearable despite painkillers.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The smaller bone on the outside of the leg may be cracked, but because the large shin bone is intact, you can still walk. We must wrap it and get an x-ray to ensure it is lined up perfectly.
Actionable Steps: Get the full-length leg x-ray tomorrow. Walk carefully using the compression bandage. Elevate your leg on a footstool when seated.

